# viberuntime

Runtime guardrail for AI-generated Python code. Gates every `subprocess.run()` call against a destructive-pattern blocklist and a network allowlist — before the OS sees the command.

[![PyPI version](https://img.shields.io/badge/pypi-v1.0.0-blue)](https://pypi.org/project/viberuntime/)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-green.svg)](LICENSE)

## Why

AI-generated code runs shell commands. Most of the time those commands are harmless. But when an agent reads a scraped page, a GitHub issue, or a third-party API response and decides to "fix dependencies" with `curl https://... | bash` — you need a gate, not a prayer.

`viberuntime` is that gate. It sits between your code and the OS, and refuses to execute commands that match known destructive patterns or reach hosts that aren't on your allowlist.

## Install

```bash
pip install viberuntime
```

No dependencies. Python 3.9+.

## Usage

Replace `subprocess.run()` with `viberuntime.run()`:

```python
from viberuntime import run, VibeRuntimeBlocked

try:
    result = run(
        "ls -la",
        allowed_hosts={"github.com", "pypi.org"},
    )
    print(result.stdout)
except VibeRuntimeBlocked as exc:
    print(f"Blocked: [{exc.signature}] {exc.reason}")
```

## What it blocks

| Pattern | Signature |
|---|---|
| Recursive deletion outside the project root | `RECURSIVE_DELETE_OUTSIDE_ROOT` |
| `curl ... \| bash`, `wget ... \| sh` | `DOWNLOAD_TO_SHELL_PIPE` |
| Base64-decoded execution | `BASE64_EXEC` |
| Port scanners (`nmap`, `masscan`, `zmap`) | `ROGUE_TOOL` |
| Brute-force tools (`hydra`, `medusa`) | `ROGUE_TOOL` |
| `mkfs`, `dd if=/dev/...` | `DISK_DESTROY` |
| Fork bomb shape | `FORK_BOMB` |
| `chmod -R 777 /etc`, `/usr`, etc. | `CHMOD_SYSTEM` |
| Overwrite of `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/shadow` | `OVERWRITE_SYSTEM` |
| Host not on the network allowlist | `NETWORK_HOST_NOT_ALLOWED` |

## Audit trail

Every gated action is appended to `.viberuntime-audit.log`:

```json
{
  "block_hash": "sha256:e3b0c44298fc...",
  "timestamp": "2026-10-02T12:00:00+00:00",
  "verdict": "BLOCKED",
  "signature": "DOWNLOAD_TO_SHELL_PIPE",
  "reason": "Download-piped-to-shell pattern detected.",
  "auditor": "viberuntime v1.0.0"
}
```

Read entries programmatically:

```python
from viberuntime import read_entries

for entry in read_entries():
    print(entry["timestamp"], entry["verdict"], entry["signature"])
```

The hash is a fingerprint — two identical commands produce the same hash, and the raw command string is never stored beyond the signature. Secrets and PII never land in the audit log.

## Configuration

```python
from viberuntime import run

run(
    command="echo hello",
    allowed_hosts={"github.com", "pypi.org", "npmjs.com"},
    project_root="/home/user/myapp",
    audit_file="/var/log/viberuntime.log",
    shell=True,
    timeout=30.0,
    check=False,
)
```

- `allowed_hosts` — network allowlist. Defaults to `github.com`, `pypi.org`, `files.pythonhosted.org`, `npmjs.com`, `registry.npmjs.org`.
- `project_root` — project root path. `rm -rf` inside it is allowed; outside is blocked.
- `audit_file` — where the audit trail is written. Defaults to `.viberuntime-audit.log`.

## What it does NOT do

- It is not a replacement for OS-level sandboxing, container isolation, or seccomp.
- It does not analyze the correctness of a command — only its safety.
- It does not run commands for you — it gates them.

`viberuntime` is a **runtime advisory layer**. Use it alongside containers, not instead of them.

